{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":491128,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d8ce6af0-742c-4502-87ca-07c0c64bcace","total_cost_usd":1.5292825000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":47293,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":40705,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":47293,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":40705,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":47293,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":47293},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":30129,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.030234,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":40705,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":47293,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.4990485000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"economy-balancer\",\n  \"flight\": {\n    \"dry_mass_kg\": 45,\n    \"fuel_capacity_kg\": 33,\n    \"thrust_n\": 4200,\n    \"fuel_burn_kgs\": 7.8,\n    \"rcs_thrust_n\": 210,\n    \"rcs_fuel_burn_kgs\": 0.39,\n    \"rotation_rate_degs\": 90\n  },\n  \"cargo\": {\n    \"base_slots\": 6,\n    \"compactor_tier\": 1\n  },\n  \"reentry\": {\n    \"heat_capacity\": 100,\n    \"heat_dissipation_s\": 5,\n    \"cargo_damage_interval_s\": 3,\n    \"unstaged_heat_multiplier\": 3.2,\n    \"off_retrograde_penalty\": 1.0\n  },\n  \"ablation\": {\n    \"fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band\": { \"suborbital\": 4.0, \"low\": 4.6, \"high\": 5.8 },\n    \"heat_cost_coefficient\": 0.00148,\n    \"heat_cost_exponent\": 2.2,\n    \"heat_index\": { \"suborbital\": 100, \"low\": 109.4, \"high\": 127.2 },\n    \"cost_curve\": {\n      \"suborbital\": [41.18, 24.18, 21.95, 23.04, 25.39, 28.33, 31.6, 35.07],\n      \"low\": [49.9, 28.92, 25.92, 26.98, 29.57, 32.88, 36.59, 40.54],\n      \"high\": [68.92, 39.07, 34.29, 35.16, 38.15, 42.15, 46.71, 51.61]\n    },\n    \"plate_capacity_pct\": 100,\n    \"optimal_pass_count\": { \"suborbital\": 3, \"low\": 3, \"high\": 3 }\n  },\n  \"landing\": {\n    \"soft_landing_ms\": 5.0,\n    \"damage_per_ms_over\": 6.0,\n    \"no_gear_multiplier\": 2.0,\n    \"fragile_multiplier\": 2.0,\n    \"parachute_drag_coefficient\": 1.75,\n    \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms\": 4.6\n  },\n  \"tow_fee\": {\n    \"free_radius_m\": 160,\n    \"max_fee_fraction\": 0.5,\n    \"curve\": \"linear\"\n  },\n  \"eva\": {\n    \"suit_mass_kg\": 20,\n    \"jetpack_thrust_n\": 200,\n    \"jetpack_fuel_kg\": 16,\n    \"jetpack_burn_kgs\": 0.5,\n    \"base_tether_slots\": 2,\n    \"magnet_hold_force_n\": 260,\n    \"magnet_range_screens\": 0.125,\n    \"latch_jerk_coefficient\": 1.0,\n    \"swing_jerk_coefficient\": 1.0\n  },\n  \"economy\": {\n    \"launch_cost\": 48,\n    \"replate_cost_per_pct\": 0.6,\n    \"repair_cost_per_pct\": 1.5,\n    \"band_value_multiplier\": { \"suborbital\": 1.0, \"low\": 2.6, \"high\": 6.0 },\n    \"size_class_base_value\": { \"small\": 22, \"medium\": 80, \"oversized\": 260 },\n    \"fragile_value_premium\": 4.0\n  },\n  \"upgrades\": [\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 180, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 48 },\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 520, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 62 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 200, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 5400 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 560, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 6800 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 240, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 9 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 700, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 12 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 260, \"effect\": \"plate_capacity_pct\", \"value\": 140 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 720, \"effect\": \"plate_capacity_pct\", \"value\": 190 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 220, \"effect\": \"parachute_drag_coefficient\", \"value\": 2.55 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 620, \"effect\": \"parachute_drag_coefficient\", \"value\": 4.2 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 190, \"effect\": \"magnet_range_screens\", \"value\": 0.1875 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 540, \"effect\": \"magnet_hold_force_n\", \"value\": 380 }\n  ],\n  \"balance_notes\": [\n    \"FULL-HOLD MASS DOUBLING (GDD 2.3.5). Spawn-weighted over the eight hand-tetherable suborbital types with the shipping tier-1 compactor (smalls crush to 1 slot, fragile smalls stay uncrushed at 2, mediums stay uncrushed at 4): E[mass] = 877/69 = 12.71 kg per piece and E[slots] = 118/69 = 1.710 slots per piece, so 12.71/1.710 = 7.43 kg per slot. Six base slots = 44.6 kg of cargo against dry_mass_kg = 45, putting the landing ship at 89.6 kg = 1.99x dry. The claim is arithmetic, not aspiration. It is also deliberately band-specific: the same six slots filled in the low band average 15.3 kg/slot (91.6 kg, 3.03x dry), which is exactly the difficulty-scales-with-success curve of GDD 2.3.7.\",\n    \"WHY dry_mass_kg IS 45 AND NOT THE RESEARCHER'S 500. The baseline lists ship dry mass 500 kg / full hold 1000 kg under 'ASSUMPTIONS THAT ARE NOT RESEARCH OUTPUTS', and it is the only way those two numbers can both be true is if a full hold weighs 500 kg - but the authored suborbital catalog only puts 44.6 kg in six slots, so at 500 kg dry a full starting hold would be a 9% mass change and GDD 2.3.5 would be dead on arrival. Base slot count is not mine to move (the GDD walkthrough puts 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' on screen) and the catalog is not mine to re-author, so dry mass is the only legal lever and I took it. CONSEQUENCE THE CODER MUST HANDLE: with the baseline's reference_area_m2 = 3.5 the staged ballistic coefficient becomes 45/(1.45*3.5) = 8.9 kg/m^2 instead of the Researcher's 98.5, which would stop the ship inside one pass and destroy the aerobraking ladder. To preserve the drag ladder the reference area must scale with the mass: A = 3.5*(45/500) = 0.315 m^2, i.e. a ~0.63 m ship. reference_area_m2 is a baseline field with no slot in my output, so I am flagging it rather than overwriting it; if the crew prefers to keep A = 3.5, then dry mass must go back to ~500 and GDD 2.3.5 has to be rewritten instead.\",\n    \"ABLATION OPTIMUM (GDD 2.3.1, 'the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes'). Model as specified: cost(band,n) = n*toll[band] + C*n*(H[band]/n)^E with C = 0.00148 and E = 2.2. Since the heat term collapses to C*H^E*n^(1-E), the continuous optimum is n* = H*(C*(E-1)/toll)^(1/E), which is linear in the heat index - so the tolls are band-gated upward (4.0 / 4.6 / 5.8) precisely to hold all three optima inside the 2-4 window despite the rising heat index. Heat indices are the Researcher's own entry-speed cubes normalised to a suborbital single pass = 100: (155.7/151.1)^3 = 1.094 -> 109.4 and (163.7/151.1)^3 = 1.272 -> 127.2. Resulting continuous optima are n* = 3.00 / 3.08 / 3.22 and the emitted cost_curve minimises at index 3 in every band. Worked example, suborbital: A = 0.00148*100^2.2 = 37.176, so cost(1) = 4.0+37.176 = 41.18, cost(2) = 8.0+16.18 = 24.18, cost(3) = 12.0+9.95 = 21.95, cost(4) = 16.0+7.04 = 23.04, cost(8) = 32.0+3.07 = 35.07. One plunge costs 1.88x the optimum and eight feathered passes cost 1.60x it, so both failure modes the GDD names are punished from opposite ends and neither is ever cheapest.\",\n    \"PARACHUTE DESCENT SPEED AT FULL HOLD (GDD 2.3.2). Terminal velocity v = sqrt(2*m*g/(rho0*Cd*A)) at rho0 = 3.0 and g = 30. Solving for the full starting hold (45 + 44.6 = 89.6 kg) at a 4.6 m/s target gives Cd*A = 2*89.6*30/(3.0*4.6^2) = 84.69 m^2; at parachute_drag_coefficient = 1.75 that is a 48.4 m^2 canopy, and it reproduces the Researcher's own parachute cross-check exactly (they got 800 m^2 for 1000 kg at 5 m/s; scaling by mass and by 1/v^2 gives 84.7 m^2). descent_speed_full_hold_ms = 4.6 sits 0.4 m/s under the soft line - near but under, as instructed. Empty the same chute gives 4.6*sqrt(45/89.6) = 3.26 m/s, so descent speed grows as the square root of mass as 2.3.2 requires.\",\n    \"STORAGE AND PARACHUTE ARE A REAL PAIR, NOT A FLAVOUR PAIR (GDD 2.3.6). Storage tier 1 (9 slots) fills to 66.9 kg of suborbital junk, mass 111.9 kg, and lands at 4.6*sqrt(111.9/89.6) = 5.14 m/s - over the line. Buying Storage without Parachute converts every full haul into a hard landing, which is the double-edged purchase the GDD promises. Parachute tier 1 (Cd 2.55) scales speed by sqrt(1.75/2.55) = 0.829, bringing that to 4.26 m/s. The worst legal haul in the shipping slice - Storage tier 2 (12 slots) full of low-band junk, 183 kg cargo, 228 kg ship - lands at 7.34 m/s bare, 6.08 m/s on Parachute tier 1, and 4.74 m/s on Parachute tier 2 (Cd 4.2). So the hardest haul the slice permits lands soft only at maximum chute, and only just.\",\n    \"LAZY RUN BREAKS EVEN (GDD 2.3.6). Cheap junk = a suborbital small at size_class_base_value 22 x band multiplier 1.0 = 22, so launch_cost 48 is 2.18 cheap pieces - inside the GDD's 'about the value of 2-3 pieces'. A lazy run is half a hold of whatever drifts past: 3 slots at the spawn-weighted rate of ~21 value/slot (smalls 22/slot, mediums 80 over 4 uncrushed slots = 20/slot) = 63. Costs are launch 48 plus re-plating the optimal 3-pass suborbital descent, 21.95% x replate_cost_per_pct 0.6 = 13.17, total 61.17. Net +1.8 - it breaks even and nothing more, which is the point. A full six-slot suborbital hold returns 126 for the same 61.17, netting ~65, and a full six-slot low-band hold returns ~330 against 63.6 of cost. Total upgrade tree is 4950, so the slice paces at roughly 4 suborbital runs to the first tier-1 part and 12-16 runs to full upgrades once the low band opens.\",\n    \"NO SHEAR UNDER STEADY TOW, PROVEN RATHER THAN ASSERTED (GDD 2.3.4). Steady-state tension in cable i is m_i * a where a = jetpack_thrust/(suit_mass + sum of tethered mass), so tension_i = m_i * F / (m_suit + sum m) < F for every possible load - the jetpack's own thrust is a hard upper bound on any single cable's steady tension, and adding a second piece lowers tension on the first. The worst legal case is one tether on the heaviest hand-tetherable piece in the entire catalog, rtg_core_shell at 96 kg: 96*200/(20+96) = 165.5 N. magnet_hold_force_n = 260 is 1.57x that and 1.30x the unreachable 200 N asymptote, so steady towing cannot shear at any load, at any tether count, at any band - which is what makes shear legible as something the player did rather than something that happened to them. STANDING CONSTRAINT: any future Jetpack thrust upgrade must stay strictly below magnet_hold_force_n (260 N base, 380 N at hand_magnet tier 2) or this proof breaks.\",\n    \"SHEAR FIRES ON JERK ONLY, AND AT A REACHABLE-BUT-DELIBERATE THRESHOLD (GDD 2.3.4). With latch_jerk_coefficient and swing_jerk_coefficient both 1.0 (tension in newtons per kg of piece mass per m/s of impulsive velocity change, absorbed over a nominal 1 s cable snap), a 34 kg bent truss latched at 8 m/s closing speed generates 272 N and shears against the 260 N base hold force, while the same latch at 5 m/s generates 170 N and holds. A 96 kg RTG shears above 2.7 m/s closing. So the rule the player learns is 'match speed before you fire, and match it harder for heavy things', and hand_magnet tier 2 (380 N) buys 46% more closing speed rather than immunity. Swing jerk uses the same coefficient against the astronaut's rate of direction change, so the same arithmetic governs yanking a load around a corner.\",\n    \"FLIGHT BUDGET, CHECKED AGAINST A FULL HOLD (charter step 1). Exhaust velocity is thrust/burn = 4200/7.8 = 538.5 m/s, giving base delta-v = 538.5*ln(78/45) = 296.1 m/s. The Researcher's suborbital ascent is 171.2 m/s ideal (injection 163.3 from r=800 to r=1000, plus 7.9 to circularise) and ~241 m/s with gravity losses over the 4.2 s burn; that leaves 4.86 kg of fuel, and with a full 44.6 kg hold aboard the remaining delta-v is 538.5*ln(94.46/89.60) = 28.4 m/s against a 6.6 m/s deorbit - enough for rendezvous drift plus the deorbit burn, with no slack for a wasted burn. Liftoff TWR is 4200/(78*30) = 1.79 on a 3.06 g world. Base delta-v is deliberately ~4 m/s short of the ~300 m/s low-band ascent, so the band gate is the Fuel Tank upgrade (tier 1 -> 390.9 m/s) rather than pilot skill, exactly as GDD 2.3.7 describes.\",\n    \"THRUSTER UPGRADES MUST SCALE fuel_burn_kgs (implementation note). fuel_burn_kgs is a single field, so raising thrust_n alone silently raises exhaust velocity and turns each Thruster tier into a free tank upgrade. The Coder should scale burn rate with thrust to hold ve = 538.5 m/s: 10.03 kg/s at tier 1 (5400 N) and 12.63 kg/s at tier 2 (6800 N). Thruster then buys acceleration only - TWR 1.94 at tier 1 with the tier-1 tank, 2.12 at tier 2 with the tier-2 tank - which keeps thrust and tank as genuinely separate purchases.\",\n    \"UNSTAGED REENTRY MULTIPLIER IS DERIVED, NOT PICKED. The Researcher's ballistic coefficients are 317 kg/m^2 unstaged (Cd 0.45) and 98.5 kg/m^2 staged (Cd 1.45), a ratio of 3.22. An unstaged ship decelerates 3.2x slower and therefore spends 3.2x longer above the heating threshold at a given depth, so unstaged_heat_multiplier = 3.2 is that ratio rather than a taste value. Combined with cargo_damage_interval_s = 3 and damage starting only at 100% heat, this preserves the GDD's rule that shallow unstaged passes are survivable while a committed unstaged entry is not. off_retrograde_penalty = 1.0 means pure cosine falloff as 2.3.1 specifies.\",\n    \"TOW FEE (GDD 2.3.3). free_radius_m = 160 is one screen at the scale the hand magnet implies (magnet_range_screens 0.125 x 160 m = a 20 m tether reach, sane for an 800 m planet). Beyond it the fee is 0.5*(d - 160)/(2513.3 - 160), where 2513.3 m = pi*R is half the circumference; the fraction is clamped to [0, 0.5], so it is zero at the pad, exactly 0.5 at the antipode, never negative, never above half, and applied to haul value only so it can never reach savings.\",\n    \"SOFT-LANDING THRESHOLD KEPT AT 5.0 DESPITE THE RESEARCHER'S OBJECTION. The baseline notes that at g = 30 a 0.42 m free fall already reaches 5 m/s, so the chute must hold the ship to contact and any bounce is automatically a hard landing, and recommends widening to ~8 m/s. I have kept 5.0 because it is a stated Key Value in GDD 2.3.2 and widening it is a design decision, not a tuning one. My mitigation is inside my own numbers: the chute is sized so the base full hold lands at 4.6 m/s with margin, and damage_per_ms_over = 6.0 makes the first metre-per-second past the line cost 6% rather than being catastrophic, so a marginal hard landing is a bruise and not a run-ender. If the human decides the last half-metre feels unfair, raising soft_landing_ms to 8 is a one-field change that does not disturb anything else in this set.\",\n    \"FRAGILE PREMIUM (GDD 2.3.7). fragile_value_premium = 4.0 means an intact fragile piece is worth four solid pieces of the same size class in the same band - the GDD's 'several'. In practice a star_tracker_head (low, small, fragile) prices at 22*2.6*4 = 229 for the 2 uncrushed slots it must occupy, 114 per slot against 57 per slot for solid low-band smalls, and a cracked_solar_array prices at 832 for 4 slots, more than an entire solid low-band hold. That is the intended shape: one fragile piece reshapes the payout, it costs double slots forever because it can never be crushed, it takes double heat and double landing damage, and its payout is gated on flying well rather than on gear - which is the GDD 4.5 hedge against an inverting difficulty curve.\",\n    \"NOTHING HERE REINTRODUCES POST-STAGE THRUST. There is no parameter in this set that gives the staged ship main or RCS authority; rcs_thrust_n and fuel_burn apply to the stage only, and the sole post-stage force available is the EVA jetpack at 200 N against the hull, which at a 89.6 kg full-hold ship is 2.2 m/s^2 and is a rescue nudge, not an engine. Staging remains one-way as GDD 2.3.1 and the section 1 pillar require.\",\n    \"MEDIUM PRICING IS SET AGAINST THE TIER-1 COMPACTOR, WHICH IS THE ONE PRICE I AM LEAST SURE OF. At tier 1 a medium rides uncrushed at 4 slots, so size_class_base_value medium = 80 works out to 20 per slot against a small's 22 - close enough that hauling the awkward heavy thing is a real decision rather than an obvious skip, and suborbital smalls and mediums happen to cost almost the same mass per slot (7.9 vs 7.75 kg), so the choice is genuinely about value and handling. The risk is on the other side of the compactor: at tier 2 a medium crushes to 2 slots and jumps to 40 per slot, nearly double a small, so mediums flip from marginal to dominant the moment tier 2 ships post-semester. I have priced for the shipping slice as instructed and flagged the tier-2 inversion here rather than pre-compensating for an upgrade that does not exist yet.\"\n  ],\n  \"catalog_concerns\": [\n    \"armstrongs_satellite, mass_kg = 480. GDD 2.3.7 says it 'roughly doubles the ship's mass on its own', but against the mass scale the suborbital catalog forces (dry_mass_kg 45, set so a full starting hold doubles the ship per GDD 2.3.5) it is 10.7x the ship, and even at Storage tier 2 full of low-band junk it is 2.1x the loaded ship. Either the satellite wants ~250-350 kg to make the doubling line true at the mass scale the rest of the catalog implies, or the GDD line needs restating as 'doubles a fully upgraded, fully laden ship'. Post-semester either way, but the two numbers cannot both stand as written.\",\n    \"tungsten_ballast_slug, size_class = small at mass_kg = 84. I understand and endorse the teaching intent - it is the piece that proves a slot is volume and a kilogram is handling - but it is the single value that most stretches the landing tuning. At tier 1 it crushes to 1 slot, so a Storage tier 2 hold of twelve of them is 1008 kg on a 45 kg ship, 23x dry, landing at 4.6*sqrt(1053/89.6) = 15.8 m/s even at Parachute tier 2. No chute value I can legally set covers that, and no other piece in the catalog comes close to that mass-per-slot. It does not ship this semester so it blocks nothing now, but if the high band is built the honest options are to lower its mass toward ~50 kg, move it to medium, or accept that one debris type is a deliberate un-landable trap.\",\n    \"bent_truss_section and spent_booster_ring, size_class = medium in the suborbital band. Against the shipping tier-1 compactor these cost 4 uncrushed slots of a 6-slot hold, so two mediums cannot fit a starting hold at all and one leaves only two slots free. That is a legitimate cost and I have priced around it (see balance_notes), but it means the starting hold's value ceiling is set almost entirely by smalls, and the player's first encounter with the towing mechanic - which the notes on both pieces describe as their whole purpose - is also the encounter that most wastes their hold. If the intent is that mediums are the mid-run target, the lever is the tier-1 crush rule or the medium slot cost, not the price, and neither is mine to move.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":393706,"ttft_stream_ms":1804,"time_to_request_ms":267,"type":"result","duration_ms":489654,"uuid":"ce08ec68-c262-443b-acd0-add79a47f46b"}
